    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-generalization-tonic-major",
      "narrative": "The II-7 chord is fully covered by the tonic major scale as an instance of harmonic generalization: 'A D-7 chord (II of C) is usually interpreted with a D dorian scale, which is a C major scale that is re-circulated from D... Hence a progression of D-7, G7, C [major] could be realized by simply playing a C major scale or a pattern on that scale' (p. 45). This is the safest form of generalization, producing no collision tones.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.45"
        }
