    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "seven-three-resolution-target",
      "narrative": "The 3rd of the V7 chord is the resolution target in the 7-3 device: 'the seventh (b7) of the first chord resolves to the third (3) of the second chord, hence the 7-3 designation' (p. 20). Coker grounds this as primarily a voice-leading and melodic device, not a suspension: 'we are now simply concerned with the smooth connection (voice-leading) of two chords, especially with respect to melodic, rather than harmonic, implications.' Two notes thus imply two chords with maximum efficiency.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.20"
        }
