      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "three-flat-nine-device",
      "narrative": "The 3-b9 device targets the dominant seventh chord exclusively: '3-b9 refers to melodic motion from the 3rd of a dominant seventh chord to the flatted 9th of the same chord, an extremely common occurrence in improvised solos' (p. 26). The motion is bidirectional ('Sometimes the soloist will move from the 3rd up to the b9, sometimes from the 3rd down to the b9') and may be executed by leap or with scalar fill. Coker chains it with 7-3: 'The 7-3 resolution by definition, ends on the 3rd of the dominant chord. Therefore that 3rd could now proceed to the b9' (p. 26).",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.26"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "device-chaining-pivot-note",
      "narrative": "The 3rd of the V7 chord serves as a pivot note linking two devices: 'The 7-3 resolution and 3-b9 present our first opportunity to observe that potential... The 7-3 resolution by definition, ends on the 3rd of the dominant chord. Therefore that 3rd could now proceed to the b9, causing the two devices to overlap, with the 3rd serving as both the resolution tone of the 7-3 and the beginning of the 3-b9' (p. 26). This chaining creates compound gestures larger than either device alone.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.26"
