    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "bebop-scale-mixolydian-variant",
      "narrative": "The mixolydian bebop scale is prescribed for dominant seventh chords: the added chromatic note is 'the half-step between the seventh degree and the octave of the first degree' (p. 33). Coker identifies this as 'the perfect anachronism to the chord quality with which it is used; that is... a major seventh against a dominant seventh' -- directly contradicting the chord's b7 quality -- yet it is '99% consistent with what we find in analyzing transcribed solos' (p. 33). Coltrane uses this bebop scale pattern 21 times in an 8-chorus solo on a 16-bar tune.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
