    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-substitution",
      "narrative": "Dominant seventh chords are the primary target for tritone substitution: 'Tri-tone substitution is the substituting, especially of dominant seventh chords, with a chord of the same type whose root is a tri-tone (diminished fifth or augmented fourth interval, or simply three whole-steps) away from the given chord, as in substituting an F#7 for a C7' (p. 82). The substitutions sound consonant because 'the listener's ear hears the given precedents for the event, instead of the conflict with the V7.'",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "coker-elements-jazz-language",
      "references": [
